---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import ImagePlaceholder from "../../components/ImagePlaceholder.astro";
import AstroNav from "../../components/AstroNav.astro";
import { ASTRO_PAGES, BASE, PIPELINE, PX, astroHref, type Step } from "../../data/astro-page";
import "../../styles/astro-pages.css";
const idx = ASTRO_PAGES.findIndex((p) => p.slug === "processing");
const next = ASTRO_PAGES[idx + 1];
---

<Layout
  title="Processing | Astrophotography | Henry Nickless"
  description="Henry Nickless's PixInsight processing pipeline for one-shot-color astrophotography, step by step."
  image="veil-nebula.jpg"
  variant="gallery"
>
  <AstroNav current="processing" />

  <section class="hero" style="padding-top: 0.5rem; padding-bottom: 1rem;">
    <span class="eyebrow">// Astrophotography</span>
    <h1>Processing</h1>
    <p class="lede">From a night of raw subs to a finished image: the PixInsight pipeline, step by step.</p>
  </section>


  <p>
    A finished image starts as anywhere from dozens to a few hundred
    individual sub-exposures, each one mostly noise with a faint, real
    signal buried in it. PixInsight combines them through pixel-by-pixel
    integration (matrix math across the whole stack), averaging with
    rejection algorithms that throw out satellite trails, cosmic-ray
    hits, and other single-frame artifacts rather than simply layering
    everything together. Signal-to-noise builds with the square root of
    the sub count, so doubling the number of subs only buys about a 40%
    improvement in signal-to-noise: the real tradeoff behind every
    session, since more subs always help but with steadily diminishing
    returns against the hours it costs to get them. Before the stack is
    combined, every frame gets normalized, scaled and offset so that
    frame-to-frame differences in sky brightness, transparency, or moon
    position don't bias the result.
  </p>

  <p>
    Everything up to that point is math. What comes after is where the
    process gets genuinely subjective; this is the part that's art, not
    measurement. Images here are captured in real, natural color, not
    narrowband-mapped or artificially recolored, and stretched through a
    histogram transform to bring out detail that's present in the linear
    data but invisible on a straight display. That step remaps what's
    already in the frame onto a range a screen can actually show; it
    doesn't invent anything that isn't there. From there, a set of
    AI-assisted tools handles gradient removal (correcting for skyglow
    and light pollution that vary across the frame), deconvolution, and
    noise reduction, cleaning up what the optics and the sky introduced
    without changing what the target actually looks like. Plate solving
    plus SPCC (Spectrophotometric Color Calibration) pins the color
    balance to real stellar spectra in the field instead of an eyeballed
    white balance, so the final color is a calibrated fit to actual data.
  </p>

  <p>
    The last pass happens in Photoshop, pulling out finer local detail
    and nudging color ever so slightly, not to invent anything, but to
    draw the eye toward the parts of the image that matter.
  </p>


  <h3 class="wf-subhead">The pipeline, step by step</h3>
  <p class="pipe-lede">
    Every image goes through the same sequence. The example here is the
    North America Nebula (NGC 7000). The order loosely follows Philippe
    Bernhard's widely shared flowchart for one-shot-color cameras: linear
    cleanup and calibration first, then stars and nebula are split and
    stretched separately before being recombined. Click any step to see
    the full screenshot.
  </p>

  {(() => {
    let n = 0;
    const card = (st: Step) => { n += 1; const no = String(n).padStart(2, "0"); return (
      <li class="pipe-card">
        {st.img ? (
          <a href={`${PX}${st.img}`} target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="pipe-thumb">
            <img src={`${PX}${st.img}`} alt={`${st.title} in PixInsight`} loading="lazy" />
          </a>
        ) : (
          <div class="pipe-thumb pipe-thumb--stat"><span class="pipe-stat">{st.stat}</span><span class="pipe-stat-label mono">{st.statLabel}</span></div>
        )}
        <div class="pipe-body">
          <span class="pipe-no mono">{no}</span>
          <h5>{st.title}</h5>
          <p>{st.note}</p>
        </div>
      </li>
    ); };
    return PIPELINE.map((group) => (
      <section class="pipe-stage">
        <h4 class="pipe-stage-title mono">{group.stage}</h4>
        {group.steps && <ol class="pipe-grid">{group.steps.map(card)}</ol>}
        {group.lanes && (
          <div class="pipe-lanes">
            {group.lanes.map((lane) => (
              <div class="pipe-lane">
                <span class="pipe-lane-name mono">{lane.name}</span>
                <ol class="pipe-grid">{lane.steps.map(card)}</ol>
              </div>
            ))}
          </div>
        )}
      </section>
    ));
  })()}

  <section class="pipe-stage">
    <h4 class="pipe-stage-title mono">Recombine &amp; finish</h4>
    <div class="pipe-finish">
      <p>
        The stretched stars are screened back over the finished starless
        image, and the result goes to Photoshop for final color
        calibration, a Camera Raw pass, and the star screen. That last
        step is where the magic happens, so it stays off the page.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>



  {next && (
    <a href={astroHref(next.slug)} class="astro-next">
      <span class="mono">Next</span>
      <strong>{next.title} →</strong>
      <span>{next.note}</span>
    </a>
  )}
</Layout>
